# Context

## Background
T084. install.sh resolves the latest release by following the
$OHYA_RELEASE_ROOT/latest redirect and taking the last path segment
as the version.

## Why This Way
GitHub answers /releases/latest with a redirect to the bare
/releases listing for a repository without a stable release
(verified live against github.com/torvalds/linux; nibzard/ohya
showed the same before v0.1.0). The stripped segment was then the
literal string "releases", which tripped the generic v-prefix
check and misreported the cause. Comparing the final URL with the
release root detects exactly that redirect, on GitHub and on
test fixtures, without guessing at tag names.

## Gotchas
- The check compares full URLs; a hypothetical tag named
  "releases" resolves to <root>/tag/releases and still reaches
  the generic version check.
- A mirror serving 200 directly at <root>/latest does not match,
  because url_effective stays <root>/latest.

